\section{The Crossref client}

The client encapsulates the HTTP session, the polite-pool identity, the
per-DOI cache, and its rate limiter.  We model it on
[[SemanticScholarProvider]] so that a reader who knows one knows the other; the
shared [[load_cache]]/[[register_cache]] calls even let it reuse the same
on-disk cache machinery.

The polite identity comes from an email address: Crossref offers a faster,
more reliable \enquote{polite} request pool to clients that identify themselves
with a [[mailto]].  We take it from the shared [[contact_email]] resolver
(\cref{sec:contact-email}), so the one [[SCHOLAR_EMAIL]] setting serves
Crossref, OpenAlex, and Unpaywall alike, while a dedicated [[CROSSREF_MAILTO]]
override is still honoured.

The address is read each time a request is built ([[mailto]]), not
kept from the constructor.  The module builds its one client when it is
imported (\cref{sec:crossref-module-interface}), and a value read then
stays for the life of the process, whatever the environment says
later.  In the test suite that was the developer's own address: pytest
imports the module while collecting, before the conftest has unset
[[SCHOLAR_EMAIL]] for a test (\cref{sec:test-configuration} in the
cache chapter), so a test's lookup carried the real address in its
[[mailto]] parameter, and only the network guard kept it from being
sent (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/241}).  Reading
the variable per request costs a dictionary lookup next to an HTTP
request, and it is how the Unpaywall client already does it.

<<the crossref client>>=
class CrossrefClient:
    """Looks up retraction/currency metadata for DOIs via Crossref."""

    name = "crossref"
    LIMITS = Limits(per_second=CROSSREF_PUBLIC_REQUESTS_PER_SECOND)
    POLITE_LIMITS = Limits(per_second=CROSSREF_POLITE_REQUESTS_PER_SECOND)

    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self._cache: dict = load_cache(self.name)
        register_cache(self.name, self._cache)
        self._limiter = limiter_for(self)
        self._session = self._create_session()
        self.last_lookup_error: str | None = None

    @property
    def mailto(self) -> str | None:
        """The polite-pool address, read from the environment now."""
        return contact_email("CROSSREF_MAILTO")

    <<crossref client methods>>
@

The session carries the same exponential back-off as the other providers
for a transient 5xx: a retraction check that trips one should retry
quietly rather than report a false \enquote{could not check}.
A 429 is not retried by the session.
[[urllib3]]'s [[Retry]] would answer it on its own, out of the limiter's
sight, and after its last attempt raise [[RetryError]] without a
response, so [[record_response]] never saw the 429 and no cooldown
began: every following DOI would run into the same burst of refused
requests.
Off the list, the 429 reaches [[record_response]], which starts a
cooldown (honouring [[Retry-After]]) that the following lookups, and
the next run, respect without asking, as Semantic Scholar's requests do
since \url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/148}.
It is a [[ScholarSession]] (\cref{ch:network}), so the requests carry
scholar's User-Agent---which names the tool and the contact address, as
Crossref's etiquette asks---and take the configured route.

<<crossref client methods>>=
def _create_session(self) -> requests.Session:
    """Create a session that retries a 5xx; a 429 goes to the limiter."""
    retry = Retry(
        total=3,
        backoff_factor=1,  # 1s, 2s, 4s delays
        status_forcelist=[500, 502, 503, 504],
        allowed_methods=["GET"],
    )
    return network.new_session(retry)
@

The pacing is the shared rate limiter's (\cref{ch:ratelimit}), the same
one the search providers use: the client declares its rate as
[[LIMITS]], like a provider, and brackets each request with
[[acquire()]] and [[record_response()]], so the requests are counted
and a 429 becomes a cooldown the \emph{next} [[scholar verify]]
respects instead of rediscovering.
The client is not a search provider, so [[scholar providers limits]]
lists it once it has a recorded state (\cref{sec:ratelimit-registry}).

The rate is Crossref's own.
Its documentation on access sets limits per pool: \enquote{Public},
with \enquote{no authentication or identification}, and
\enquote{Polite}, for requests that \enquote{include your email address
in the mailto parameter or agent header}.
Its table gives the public pool a rate limit of 5 and a concurrency
limit of 1, the polite pool 10 and 3; the rate limits \enquote{are
included in the results headers under x-rate-limit-limit and
x-rate-limit-interval}, the most requests allowed in that interval, and
\enquote{if you exceed the rate limits, you will receive a 429 HTTP
response}
(\url{https://www.crossref.org/documentation/retrieve-metadata/rest-api/access-and-authentication/},
last updated 2025-10-16, read 2026-11-20).
A response recorded on 2026-11-20 without a [[mailto]] announced the
public figures: [[x-rate-limit-limit: 5]], [[x-rate-limit-interval:
1s]], [[x-concurrency-limit: 1]].
The client used to declare ten requests per second whatever the pool,
twice what an anonymous client is allowed
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/270}).

So the client declares both rates and paces each request at the rate
of the pool it goes to, which depends on whether it carries a
[[mailto]]; the address is read per request (above), so the rate is
chosen per request too, as Semantic Scholar chooses between its keyed
and anonymous rates.
The limiter does not read the [[x-rate-limit-*]] headers: its header
sync knows only the [[X-RateLimit-Remaining]] and
[[X-RateLimit-Reset]] pair (\cref{ch:ratelimit}), and declaring the
documented rates needs no change there.
The concurrency limits are met by the way the client is used: one
process sends one request at a time.
Several processes running at once are not coordinated; each paces
itself.

<<constants>>=
# Crossref REST endpoint for single-work lookups, and request tuning.
CROSSREF_WORKS_URL = "https://api.crossref.org/works"
CROSSREF_TIMEOUT = 30
# Crossref's documented rate limits per pool (requests per second).
CROSSREF_PUBLIC_REQUESTS_PER_SECOND = 5.0  # no mailto
CROSSREF_POLITE_REQUESTS_PER_SECOND = 10.0  # with a mailto
<<crossref client methods>>=
def _acquire(self) -> None:
    """Pace and count one request at the rate of its pool.

    Raises `RateLimited` while a persistent cooldown is active.
    """
    self._limiter.limits = (
        self.POLITE_LIMITS if self.mailto else self.LIMITS
    )
    self._limiter.acquire()
@

