\subsection{Fetching a work's currency metadata}

The lookup is the heart of the module.  The DOI must be URL-encoded before it is
spliced into the path: DOIs routinely contain slashes (and occasionally spaces
or other reserved characters), which would otherwise be read as extra path
segments and produce a spurious 404.

The return contract is the checked/unknown distinction from the overview: a
dictionary with [[updated_by]], [[newer_versions]], and [[preprints]] lists
when Crossref served the record (HTTP~200), and [[None]] when it did not
(missing DOI, 404, 429, or error).  A 404 is cached as [[None]] so an
unregistered DOI is not retried every run; a 429 or transport error is left
uncached so a later run can try again.  While the limiter refuses (a
remembered cooldown), the lookup is likewise undetermined; the limiter
has already said so once, so we stay quiet rather than warn per DOI.

[[None]] thus stands both for an answer (no DOI to ask about, or
Crossref has no record of it) and for a check that failed.
A paper whose check failed keeps its currency fields as they were
([[annotate_papers]] below), so it stays \enquote{never checked},
which is right; but a run that failed for every paper (a broken TLS
set-up, say) used to end with \enquote{No retractions or corrections
found} as if it had checked them all.
So the client keeps the reason the last lookup failed in
[[last_lookup_error]], as the providers' DOI lookups and Unpaywall do
(\cref{sec:lookup-outcome}): reset first thing, set on exactly the
uncached failure paths, in the words of
[[network.request_failure_reason]] (\cref{sec:request-failure-reason}),
which never quote a [[requests]] exception and hence never the URL
with its [[mailto]] address.
The warnings quote the exception, but through
[[network.redact_secrets]], which hides that address.
The commands count these reasons (\cref{sec:crossref-check-failures}).

The cache key carries an explicit schema version.  When the [[preprints]]
key was added to the result dictionary, entries cached under the old
key shape lacked it, and a reader could not tell \enquote{no preprint
relation} from \enquote{cached before the key existed}.  Bumping
[[CURRENCY_CACHE_VERSION]] orphans the old entries instead---they
simply miss and are refetched, which is cheap---so every served result
is guaranteed to have the full schema.

<<constants>>=
# Bump when the fetch_currency result schema changes, so stale cache
# entries miss instead of being served with keys absent.
CURRENCY_CACHE_VERSION = 2
<<crossref client methods>>=
def fetch_currency(self, doi: str | None) -> dict | None:
    """Return currency metadata for ``doi``, or ``None`` if undetermined.

    On an HTTP 200 response, returns a dict with ``updated_by``,
    ``newer_versions``, and ``preprints`` lists (each possibly empty).
    Returns ``None`` when the DOI is missing, not found, rate-limited,
    or the request fails. The last two set `last_lookup_error`.
    """
    self.last_lookup_error = None
    if not doi:
        return None

    cache_key = ("currency", CURRENCY_CACHE_VERSION, doi)
    if cache_key in self._cache:
        logger.debug("crossref: cache hit for DOI %s", doi)
        return self._cache[cache_key]
    if not self._fetch_work(doi):
        return None
    return self._cache[cache_key]
@

The request itself is shared with the abstract lookup
(\cref{sec:crossref-abstract}): both read the same work record, so
[[_fetch_work]] asks once and caches what each of them reads from the
answer ([[_remember_work]]).
It reports only whether Crossref answered---with the record or with a
404---or the lookup failed, in which case nothing is cached and
[[last_lookup_error]] says why.

<<crossref client methods>>=
def _fetch_work(self, doi: str) -> bool:
    """Ask Crossref for `doi`'s record and cache what we read from it.

    Returns True when Crossref answered (with the record, or a 404),
    False when the lookup failed; then `last_lookup_error` says why.
    """
    try:
        self._acquire()
        url = f"{CROSSREF_WORKS_URL}/{quote(doi, safe='')}"
        mailto = self.mailto
        params = {"mailto": mailto} if mailto else {}
        response = self._session.get(
            url,
            params=params,
            timeout=CROSSREF_TIMEOUT,
        )
        self._limiter.record_response(response)

        if response.status_code == 404:
            # DOI unknown to Crossref: cache the miss to avoid re-lookups.
            self._remember_work(doi, None)
            return True
        if response.status_code == 429:
            logger.warning("crossref: rate limited for DOI %s", doi)
            self.last_lookup_error = network.http_status_reason(429)
            return False

        response.raise_for_status()
        self._remember_work(doi, response.json().get("message", {}))
        return True

    except RateLimited as e:
        self.last_lookup_error = e.reason
        return False
    except requests.exceptions.HTTPError as e:
        logger.warning(
            "crossref: lookup error for %s: %s",
            doi, network.redact_secrets(e),
        )
        self.last_lookup_error = network.request_failure_reason(e)
        return False
    except Exception as e:
        logger.warning(
            "crossref: lookup failed for %s: %s",
            doi, network.redact_secrets(e),
        )
        self.last_lookup_error = network.request_failure_reason(e)
        return False
@

The DOI is percent-encoded whole, its slash included
([[safe='']]), and the [[mailto]] goes to [[requests]] as a parameter,
so no character of the DOI can end the path or start a query.
Crossref's documentation is of two minds about the slash: its page on
DOI suffixes says \enquote{Do not encode forward slash / when resolving
DOIs or retrieving metadata from our REST API}, with [[#]], [[<]] and
[[>]] encoded in its example
(\url{https://www.crossref.org/documentation/member-setup/constructing-your-dois/suffixes-containing-special-characters/},
last updated 2021-12-03, read 2026-11-23), while the REST API's own
documentation says \enquote{You should always url-encode DOIs and
parameter values when using the API}
(\url{https://github.com/crossref/rest-api-doc}, read 2026-11-23).
The encoded slash is answered: the responses recorded below were asked
for as [[works/10.7717%2Fpeerj.4375]].
So the request stays as it is; let's verify that a DOI holding the
characters with a meaning in a URL (\cref{sec:request-targets} in the
cache module) arrives whole and that the address is the only
parameter.

