\section{Reading a work's abstract}
\label{sec:crossref-abstract}

The work record we fetch for the currency check can carry the work's
abstract as well.
Crossref's REST API documentation says so in as many words: the API
exposes \enquote{funding data, license information, post-publication
updates, ORCID and ROR IDs, and abstracts}
(\url{https://www.crossref.org/documentation/retrieve-metadata/rest-api/},
read 2026-11-20), and its Swagger description lists [[abstract]] as a
string field of a work (\url{https://api.crossref.org/swagger-docs},
version 4.10.3, read 2026-11-20).
Members deposit it: \enquote{You can include JATS-formatted abstracts
with your metadata records. All abstracts registered with us will be
included in the metadata distributed through our metadata outputs}
(\url{https://www.crossref.org/documentation/principles-practices/best-practices/abstracts/},
read 2026-11-20).
So the field is present only when the publisher deposited an abstract,
and then it is JATS XML, not plain text.
A recorded response shows both (2026-11-20, [[GET
https://api.crossref.org/works/10.7717%2Fpeerj.4375]] and
[[.../10.1109%2FCVPR.2016.90]], no [[mailto]] sent): the first record
holds its abstract as one [[<jats:p>]] element, the second has no
[[abstract]] key at all.

The terms differ from the rest of the record.
The same REST API page says that \enquote{almost none of the metadata is
subject to copyright, and you may use it for any purpose. Some abstracts
contained in the metadata may be subject to copyright by publishers or
authors.}
Scholar stores an abstract in the user's own session for screening, as
it stores those the search providers return; the session's
[[Paper.sources]] names Crossref among the sources of the record.

Enrichment (\cref{enrich-module}) asks Crossref for an abstract only
for papers the search providers before it (all but Scopus) left
without one.
No second request is needed for that: the abstract is read from the
same response as the currency check, cached in an entry of its own
beside the currency result ([[_remember_work]] above).
We chose that side entry over adding an [[abstract]] key to the
currency result and bumping [[CURRENCY_CACHE_VERSION]].
A bump would orphan every cached currency result, and since the
currency check runs on every paper of every [[scholar enrich]], the
next run would ask Crossref again for every DOI of the session,
including the papers that have had an abstract all along.
With a side entry the currency results keep serving; a DOI cached
before the abstract was kept has a currency result but no abstract
entry, and only when enrichment asks for its abstract---a paper still
without one---is it fetched again, which refreshes both entries.
The entry is keyed apart from the currency results, so it cannot be
mistaken for one; it holds the raw text or [[None]], built-in types
only, like the rest of the cache file.

<<abstract cache key>>=
def abstract_cache_key(doi: str) -> tuple:
    """The cache key of `doi`'s abstract as Crossref sent it."""
    return ("abstract", doi)
@

\subsection{From JATS to plain text}

A JATS abstract is a small XML document: paragraphs ([[<jats:p>]]),
possibly sections with titles, line breaks, formulas, and inline
elements such as [[<jats:italic>]] or [[<jats:sub>]].
We want the words as a reader sees them.
Turning such markup into text is shared with Europe PMC's abstracts, so
it is done by [[markup_to_text]] (\cref{sec:markup-to-text}): block
elements become spaces, inline ones vanish, one version of a formula is
kept, entities are decoded after the tags are gone.
It is not done by [[clean_metadata_text]], which every title passes
through (\cref{sec:clean-provider-text}) and which removes only a fixed
list of inline tags: turning block elements into spaces is right for an
abstract but not something to do to every title.

One rule is Crossref's own.
A title [[Abstract]] at the start, alone or as the first title of a
first section ([[<jats:sec><jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>]]), is a
heading that repeats what the field is, and it is dropped; any other
title (a structured abstract's \enquote{Background},
\enquote{Methods}) is part of the text and stays.
The recorded abstract above has no such title; the rule is ours, a
guard for records that carry one.

<<constants>>=
# A leading <title>Abstract</title>, also inside the first <sec>,
# repeats the field's name.
JATS_LEADING_ABSTRACT_TITLE = re.compile(
    r"^\s*((?:<(?:jats:)?sec\b[^<>]*>\s*)*)"
    r"<(?:jats:)?title\b[^<>]*>\s*abstract\s*</(?:jats:)?title\s*>",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
<<abstract normalization helpers>>=
def jats_to_text(raw: str) -> str:
    """Return the plain text of a JATS-marked abstract.

    Drops a leading `<title>Abstract</title>` (also as the first
    section's title), then reads the rest with `markup_to_text`.
    """
    return markup_to_text(JATS_LEADING_ABSTRACT_TITLE.sub(r"\1", raw))
@

Let's verify the Crossref rule, and that the shared reading applies;
the shared rules have their own tests.

<<test functions>>=
class TestJatsToText:
    """jats_to_text keeps the words and drops the markup (#260)."""

    def test_paragraphs_are_separated_by_a_space(self):
        raw = "<jats:p>First part.</jats:p><jats:p>Second part.</jats:p>"
        assert jats_to_text(raw) == "First part. Second part."

    def test_leading_abstract_title_is_dropped(self):
        raw = "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title><jats:p>Text.</jats:p>"
        assert jats_to_text(raw) == "Text."

    def test_abstract_title_of_the_first_section_is_dropped(self):
        raw = (
            "<jats:sec><jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>"
            "<jats:p>Text.</jats:p></jats:sec>"
        )
        assert jats_to_text(raw) == "Text."

    def test_other_section_titles_stay(self):
        raw = (
            "<jats:sec><jats:title>Background</jats:title>"
            "<jats:p>Why.</jats:p></jats:sec>"
        )
        assert jats_to_text(raw) == "Background Why."

    def test_a_later_abstract_word_stays(self):
        raw = "<jats:p>Intro.</jats:p><jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>"
        assert jats_to_text(raw) == "Intro. Abstract"
@

\subsection{Looking an abstract up}

[[fetch_abstract]] follows the contract of [[fetch_currency]]: the text
when Crossref answered with one, [[None]] otherwise, and
[[last_lookup_error]] set exactly when the lookup failed, so that the
enrichment tally (\cref{sec:enrich-lookup-tally}) can tell a record
without an abstract from a lookup that did not happen.
A cached abstract entry is an answer, whether text or [[None]];
without one we fetch the record, which also refreshes the currency
result.
An abstract that is only markup (an empty paragraph) leaves no text,
and that is no abstract either: [[None]], not an empty string.

<<crossref client methods>>=
def fetch_abstract(self, doi: str | None) -> str | None:
    """Return the plain-text abstract Crossref holds for `doi`, or None.

    None when there is no DOI, Crossref has no record or no abstract
    (or one without text) for it, or the lookup failed; the last sets
    `last_lookup_error`.
    """
    self.last_lookup_error = None
    if not doi:
        return None
    key = abstract_cache_key(doi)
    if key not in self._cache and not self._fetch_work(doi):
        return None
    raw = self._cache.get(key)
    return (jats_to_text(raw) or None) if raw else None
@

Enrichment asks every source the same question, [[get_paper_by_doi]],
and merges the answer into the paper (\cref{enrich-module}).
Our answer is a paper with the DOI and the abstract and nothing else:
the record holds a title and authors too, but the search providers
have filled those already, and this lookup is there for the abstract.
Its [[sources]] name Crossref, so the merge records where the abstract
came from.
An empty text would fill nothing, so it is no answer.

<<crossref client methods>>=
def get_paper_by_doi(self, doi: str | None) -> Paper | None:
    """Return a paper holding only `doi` and its Crossref abstract.

    None when Crossref has no abstract for the DOI or the lookup
    failed (see `fetch_abstract`).
    """
    abstract = self.fetch_abstract(doi)
    if not abstract:
        return None
    return Paper(
        title="", authors=[], doi=doi, abstract=abstract,
        sources=[self.name],
    )
@

The module exposes the lookup over its shared client, and the client
itself for the enrichment loop, which reads [[last_lookup_error]] from
the object it asked.

<<module-level abstract interface>>=
def fetch_abstract(doi: str | None) -> str | None:
    """Look up the plain-text abstract of a DOI (see CrossrefClient)."""
    return _client.fetch_abstract(doi)


def enrichment_source() -> "CrossrefClient":
    """The shared client, as an abstract source for `scholar.enrich`."""
    return _client
@

The tests replay the two recorded records, trimmed to the fields the
code reads ([[abstract]], [[updated-by]] and [[relation]], of which the
records hold no notice and no relation we read, so they are left out)
plus the DOI and title; the abstract is cut to its first two sentences.
They check what the overview promised: the abstract arrives as plain
text, one request serves both the abstract and the currency check in
either order, a record without an abstract is an answer and is cached,
a currency result cached before the abstract was kept costs one
request for the abstract and none for the currency, and a failure is
neither cached nor silent.

